# aGTM Custom Template

- Version 1.5
- Autor: Andi Petzoldt <andi@petzoldt.net>
- Last Update: 21.10.2026

## Description

This template sets the Google (and Microsoft) Consent Mode signals for GTM.

## Changes in 1.5

- **Fired by `aGTM_consent_update`, the tag sends only a Consent Mode Update**
  (F-242). Give the page-load tag a second trigger on that Custom Event: a
  decision changed on the same page — a revocation included — then reaches
  Consent Mode at once instead of on the next page load. No Default, region,
  `wait_for_update` or `gtagSet` on that run; Microsoft UET gets
  `consent update`. New permission: read the dataLayer key `event`.
  The tag firing option must be **Once per event** — with Once per page GTM
  never runs the second trigger.
- Microsoft Consent Mode (`ms_consent_mode`) reworked and documented. Measured
  against the live `bat.bing.com/bat.js` (2026-08-19), not inferred from the
  Microsoft docs alone:
  - **`ad_storage` now decides whether anything is sent at all.** It is the only
    signal UET evaluates. The first consent push sets `uetConfig.consent.enabled`
    before the payload is even inspected, which turns off both UET's own
    EEA/UK/CH enforcement (where its default is DENIED) and `preEnforce()`,
    which otherwise reads Google Consent Mode out of `window.google_tag_data.ics`
    ~1.5 s after load. A payload without `ad_storage` would therefore disable
    that machinery AND leave the visitor on granted. When `ad_storage` is not
    configured, this tag sends nothing — the safer branch, not a blank one.
  - Only the literal values `granted` and `denied` count as configured. The
    signal fields accept a GTM variable; an empty or unexpected value used to be
    forwarded, and `adStorageAllowed` is computed as `'denied' !== ad_storage`,
    so anything else reads as granted.
  - The "Update after Default" baseline covers all three signals unconditionally.
    It used to be built from the signals that happened to be set, so `ad_storage`
    could be missing from the very default the mode exists for. UET has no region
    parameter, so that baseline is global.
  - `cm_wait` is passed on as UET's `wait_for_update` (UET caps it at 10 000 ms).
  - Clarity is all-or-nothing: Microsoft documents both `consentv2` fields as
    required, so the call is made only when `ad_storage` and `analytics_storage`
    are both configured.
  - The option was undocumented until now; see the README, including a section on
    when the checkbox is worth switching on at all.
- Tests: nine `___TESTS___` scenarios cover the Microsoft branch (no push without
  `ad_storage`, non-state values, all three signals, update mode, the complete
  denied baseline, `wait_for_update`, Clarity all-or-nothing, and the Clarity
  queue fallback).
- Cleanup: removed two dead `require()`s that the F-42 permission sweep had missed —
  `createQueue('dataLayer')` (used only in a commented-out line, superseded by
  `aGTM.f.fire`) and `makeTableMap` (never used at all).
- Permissions narrowed: with the dead `createQueue('dataLayer')` gone, the template no
  longer needs a direct read/write `access_globals` grant on the global `dataLayer`, so
  that entry was dropped. `write_data_layer` stays — that is the permission `gtagSet`
  requires for `url_passthrough` / `ads_data_redaction`, and its `keyPatterns` cover
  exactly those two keys.
- No behaviour change: the consent signals still go out through the
  `setDefaultConsentState` / `updateConsentState` template APIs.
- Note when re-importing: GTM will show the narrower permission set as a permission diff.

## Changes in 1.4

- Fix: guard against `aGTM.d.consent` being undefined (aGTM not loaded yet) so
  the tag no longer throws and skips setting the default consent state on the
  Consent Initialization trigger (fail-open closed).
- Fix: the consent-check fallback now runs in production (not only in debug) and
  re-reads the consent it just wrote.
- Fix: in "Update after Default" mode the region is now applied to the (denied)
  default via `setDefaultConsentState`; it stays off `updateConsentState`
  (which has no region parameter). A configured `cm_regions` is no longer ignored.
- New: optional `cm_grant_outside` — emit a global granted default (for the
  managed signals) so visitors outside the configured regions are granted by
  default (Google's two-default pattern).
- Tests: added `___TESTS___` regression scenarios (F-41) covering the fail-open
  guard, granted pass-through, update vs. default mode, region-scoped denied
  default in "update after default", the consent-check re-read, and the
  `not_set` signal omission.

